<p>A U.S.-brokered 10-day ceasefire between Israel and Lebanon took effect late on April 16, after President Trump said he secured the agreement through talks with Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and Lebanese President Joseph Aoun. U.S. officials said the pause is meant to create space for talks on a more permanent security and peace arrangement, and Washington said the truce could be extended if both sides agree. Several outlets also said the deal matters to wider U.S.-Iran diplomacy, because fighting between Israel and Hezbollah had threatened broader regional de-escalation efforts. The first hours of the ceasefire brought both hope and strain. Lebanese media and regional outlets described celebrations in parts of Lebanon as displaced people began heading south, while the Lebanese army soon reported what it called Israeli violations after the truce took effect. In some reports, Hezbollah signaled support for the ceasefire, though several sources said the group had not clearly committed to all of its terms and tied its compliance to Israel fully halting attacks. Trump also said he expected or hoped to host Israeli and Lebanese leaders at the White House soon. Multiple reports described that as a potentially rare, or even first, direct meeting at that level between the two countries, offering at least a small opening for diplomacy after weeks of war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/6FEAKTEEeFDMmS5Sr83IOSnfPAchIZI_DM3rDm_dPYfOCGp0pF3IJAUbErRiku4haEooG9mQQK-3mV4u-mbisjtPJA8Lw1WuAeUktxW3Ip6NqzF0CICbJBvgTvl5OAz-BuDoM1WbBcReMnwjat2EsyS50chJJCreYrU39HnV4iKAeqm3Mli6XzvqMHeLtt0wxyuLSgLuWn71Si_S' alt='Beirut, Lebanon, on Thursday. Hundreds of thousands of Lebanese have been forced by the fighting to leave their homes.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Ceasefire terms: The truce began at 5 p.m. Eastern Time, midnight local time in Israel and Lebanon, and U.S. statements said either side could seek an extension by mutual agreement.</li><li>Return movement: As the truce took hold, displaced residents were reported streaming south toward their homes, though the Lebanese army warned some people not to return yet because of ongoing security concerns.</li><li>Hezbollah stance: Hezbollah lawmaker Ibrahim Moussawi said the group would respect the ceasefire if Israel fully abided by it, including a total cessation of hostilities and a withdrawal from occupied areas, according to RT&#x27;s reporting.</li></ul><blockquote>These two Leaders have agreed that in order to achieve PEACE between their Countries, they will formally begin a 10 Day CEASEFIRE at 5 P.M. EST - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: Washington presented the 10-day pause as a bridge to talks on a permanent security and peace agreement and said it could be extended if both sides agree. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/ten-day-israel-lebanon-ceasefire-may-be-extended-mutual-agreement-us-says'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Lebanese army: The army reported a number of Israeli violations after the truce began and warned residents in the south not to return immediately because of what it described as acts of aggression. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/10-day-israel-lebanon-truce-begins-lebanese-army-warns-violations-0'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Hezbollah: In some reporting, Hezbollah voiced support but tied its commitment to Israel stopping attacks, signaling conditional backing rather than an unqualified endorsement. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/10-day-ceasefire-between-israel-lebanon-goes-into-effect/live-76802532'>DW</a>)</li><li>France: President Emmanuel Macron warned that the Lebanon-Israel ceasefire may already be unraveling, reflecting European concern about how fragile the deal is. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-us-war-lebanon-army-israeli-ceasefire-violation-donald-trump-west-asia-conflict-live-updates-april-17-2026/article70872084.ece'>The Hindu<